  "body": "Stacked on #1181 (on #1163, #1144, #1139, #1123, #560, #551)\n\nFork synced at v0.18.0.\n\n## What changed\n\nA wire EDM can now state an **angle-word taper dialect** in place of U/V moves. The control's behaviour goes in the machine file as data, so the verify can judge the lean the control will actually cut.\n\n- **Machine data** (`post::machine::edm_taper`, new): `edm.taper_angle { positive: top_left|top_right, corner: mitre|cone }`. Both values are enumerated words with no default. A garbled word refuses the file. An unstated one is refused by name at generation. The angle code is `edm.codes.taper_angle`, a template with `{angle}`.\n- **Generator** (`motion::wire_edm::taper`): a `WireCorner` rule.\n  - `Mitre` is #536's path. On the fixture its CL equals #536's golden byte for byte (pinned).\n  - `Cone` pivots the wire about each top-plane corner and holds the lean at the angle. The lower end runs on an arc of radius `H tan a`, laid as posed moves at most 2 degrees apart.\n  - Under `cone`, a lead-in that meets a corner is refused by name.\n- **Door** (`app::operation::wire_op::corner_rule`): a U/V machine keeps the mitre. An angle-word machine uses its stated rule. An incomplete rule is refused by name, and so is a 4-axis cut, because one angle cannot state two profiles. The rule is stamped only for angle-word machines, so U/V stamps do not change.\n- **Post** (`app::posting_wire::angled`, `wire-iso.cps`):\n  - Each move's X Y is written on the programmed plane, which is the work's top. The angle word is passed as `PARAM WireAngle`, signed by the side rule and computed from the path. No U/V words are written.\n  - The cone's turning moves are not sent, because the control makes them itself.\n  - `taper_on` and the angle word are written before the lead-in, and `taper_off` before the lead-out.\n  - A machine that states neither `uv` nor `edm.taper_angle` is refused by name.\n  - The new PARAMs are listed in `docs/CNC-INTERMEDIATE.md`.\n- The decision record is `docs/decisions/1164-wire-taper-angle-word.md`.\n\n## How tested\n\n- `crates/app/tests/wire_taper_angle.rs` (new):\n  - `an_angle_word_punch_posts_its_golden_with_the_mitre_rule`: new NC golden `tests/golden/nc/wire-iso/wire_taper_angle_punch.nc`; the CL equals `wire_taper_punch.cl`; the sign flips under `top_right`.\n  - `the_cone_rule_turns_the_wire_about_each_corner`: the lean is held at 3 degrees through every corner, and the NC equals the mitre golden except for the note line.\n  - `a_corner_the_stated_rule_gouges_blocks_the_post`: on a 3-degree drafted punch with sharp corners, the mitre rule verifies clean and posts. The cone rule's rounded lower corners gouge it, so the post is blocked with `wire gouge`.\n  - `an_incomplete_dialect_and_a_four_axis_angle_cut_are_refused_by_name`: a missing corner rule, a missing side rule, neither uv nor angle, and 4-axis on an angle machine.\n- `motion` unit test `the_cone_rule_holds_the_lean_and_refuses_a_lead_into_a_corner`; `post` unit test `taper_angle_words_parse_and_garbled_ones_refuse_the_file`.\n- U/V goldens: `wire_taper`, `wire_4axis`, `wire_post`, `wire_contour` and `wire_verify` pass with their goldens unedited.\n- Gate on the final head 39d014d8: `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/issue/537-wire-4axis` green (3 steps, 0 failed, 11.2 min); the wire suites by hand (wire_taper 5, wire_4axis 6, wire_post 5, wire_contour 7, wire_verify 3, wire_taper_angle 4: all pass); `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post -p camcraft-motion -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No real EDM control (D10). The angle code and dialect words are invented on the synthetic fixture.\n- How a real control ramps the lean over the lead-in and lead-out is modelled as a linear ramp, the same as #536. The decision record states this.\n- The `arc` corner rule (lean change rounded over a stated radius) is not offered.\n- No UI page field for `edm.taper_angle`. It is machine-file data only, like `edm.codes`.\n\nChangelog: Wire EDM: a machine can state an angle-word taper dialect (edm.taper_angle: side rule and mitre/cone corner rule). The wire post writes the angle word, and the verify judges the lean the stated corner rule cuts.\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #1164\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
